<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<ValidationRule xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        THIS NOTE LIVES IN A COMMENT, NOT IN <description>. ValidationRule.Description is
        capped at 255 characters; a comment is repo-only and is never sent to the Metadata
        API. It must sit INSIDE the root element. A comment placed ABOVE <ValidationRule>
        breaks `sf` deploy at conversion with a misleading "unable to find matching parent xml
        file" error.

        =====================================================================================
        ADDED 2026-08-20 (Tranche 2, Workstream A4). THIS FILE CARRIES THE SHARED RATIONALE
        FOR ALL FOUR A4 SANITY RULES ON THIS OBJECT; THE OTHER THREE POINT HERE.
        =====================================================================================
        Siblings: DPEG_Counter_Price_Is_Positive, Closing_Period_Days_Not_Negative,
        Due_Diligence_Days_Not_Negative.

        WHAT A SANITY RULE IS FOR. These four refuse values that are arithmetically impossible
        rather than commercially unwise. A counter price of zero or below is not a hard
        negotiating position, it is a typo or a failed import; a negative day count is not a
        term, it is a data defect. None of them expresses a policy about how DPEG negotiates.

        =====================================================================================
        🔴 WHY NOT(ISBLANK(...)) COMES FIRST, AND WHY THAT IS NOT DEFENSIVE PADDING.
        =====================================================================================
        A BLANK counter price is the NORMAL state: most offers are never countered. On a
        Currency field ISBLANK distinguishes empty from a deliberately entered 0, and only the
        blank test can make that distinction, so the guard is what separates "no counter yet"
        from "a counter of zero". Without it this rule would refuse every offer that has not
        been countered, which is nearly all of them.

        =====================================================================================
        🔴 WHY FOUR NARROW RULES RATHER THAN THE TWO PAIRED RULES THE TRANCHE 2 REQUIREMENTS
        DOC ENUMERATES. THIS IS A DELIBERATE DIVERGENCE FROM §2 A4's FILE LIST.
        =====================================================================================
        The doc proposes one Counter_Price_Is_Positive covering BOTH counter fields and one
        Term_Days_Are_Not_Negative covering BOTH day fields. A paired rule can carry only ONE
        errorDisplayField, so half the time the inline error renders under the field the user
        did NOT touch: type minus 30 into Due Diligence Days and the message appears beside
        Closing Period Days. A user cannot act on that, and an unactionable error is the
        failure mode an errorDisplayField exists to prevent.
        THE COST OF SPLITTING IS TWO EXTRA FILES AND TWO EXTRA FORMULA EVALUATIONS PER SAVE
        on an object with no other rules. That is cheaper than a misdirected error.
        ⚠ IF THESE ARE EVER RE-MERGED, DROP errorDisplayField ENTIRELY rather than picking
        one of the two fields. A page level error is honest; an error pointing at the wrong
        field is not. Broker_Lookup_Is_Off_Market_Only on Disposition__c is the in repo
        precedent for omitting the tag on purpose.

        =====================================================================================
        NO ISCHANGED / ISNEW GUARD ON ANY OF THE FOUR. Each is an INVARIANT, not a transition,
        and each is SELF CLEARING: the remedy, correcting or clearing the offending number, is
        itself a permitted save. An unguarded rule therefore cannot trap a row here.
        ⚠ SYSTEM_MODE DML does NOT bypass validation rules, so a bad row would also refuse
        machine writes until corrected. That is the intended behaviour and is why the remedy
        must stay permitted.
        =====================================================================================
        MEASURED IMPACT AT CREATION: ZERO ROWS. Queried on usman-dpeg, 2026-08-20:
          SELECT COUNT(Id) FROM Disposition_Offer__c -> 0
        The object is empty, so all four rules refuse nothing that exists. 🔴 That measurement
        ages; re-run at deploy time.

        ⚠ SINGLE MODULE OBJECT. Unlike LOI__c and Contract_Review__c, Disposition_Offer__c has
        no record types and serves the disposition module only, so none of these four rules
        can reach an acquisition record. No record type test is needed or wanted.
    -->
    <fullName>Buyer_Counter_Price_Is_Positive</fullName>
    <active>true</active>
    <description>Blocks a zero or negative Buyer Counter Price. A blank counter is legitimate and is left alone; only a counter that has been entered is checked. Split from the paired rule the design proposed so the error anchors to the field actually at fault.</description>
    <errorConditionFormula>AND(
    NOT(ISBLANK(Buyer_Counter_Price__c)),
    Buyer_Counter_Price__c &lt;= 0
)</errorConditionFormula>
    <errorMessage>Buyer Counter Price must be greater than zero. Leave it blank if the buyer has not countered; enter the countered amount if they have.</errorMessage>
    <errorDisplayField>Buyer_Counter_Price__c</errorDisplayField>
</ValidationRule>
